---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="10-decomposition"
  lessonSlug="03-refactor-monolith"
  hook="A 12-line function that does three jobs. Split it into three named one-job helpers + a thin orchestrator. Behavior must be preserved."
>
  <h2>Before — the monolith</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="Works, but tangled"
    code={`import math

def analyze_number_OLD(n):
    is_even = (n % 2 == 0)
    if n < 2:
        is_prime = False
    else:
        is_prime = True
        for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):
            if n % i == 0:
                is_prime = False
                break
    total = 0
    for c in str(abs(n)):
        total += int(c)
    return {"n": n, "even": is_even, "prime": is_prime, "digit_sum": total}

print(analyze_number_OLD(12))
print(analyze_number_OLD(17))`}
  />

  <h2>After — three helpers + thin describe</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`import math

def is_even(n):
    return n % 2 == 0

def is_prime(n):
    if n < 2:
        return False
    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):
        if n % i == 0:
            return False
    return True

def digit_sum(n):
    total = 0
    for c in str(abs(n)):
        total += int(c)
    return total

def describe(n):
    return {
        "n": n,
        "even": is_even(n),
        "prime": is_prime(n),
        "digit_sum": digit_sum(n),
    }

for x in [12, 17, -42, 1, 0]:
    print(describe(x))`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Behavior preservation">
    <p>
      The autograder runs the same inputs through both <code>describe(n)</code>
      and the original <code>analyze_number_OLD(n)</code> and verifies they
      agree. Refactoring means "improve the structure without changing the
      behavior."
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Why split it?">
    <p>
      <code>is_prime</code> is now reusable anywhere. The orchestrator reads
      like the problem statement instead of a math soup. Each helper can be
      tested in isolation. From Think Python §3.11: <em>"Creating a new
      function gives you an opportunity to name a group of statements."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
